+% Generated by roxygen2: do not edit by hand
+% Please edit documentation in R/RunIREA.R
+\name{RunIREA}
+\alias{RunIREA}
+\alias{RunIREA.default}
+\alias{RunIREA.Seurat}
+\title{Analyse cytokine responses or immune-cell polarization}
+\usage{
+RunIREA(object, ...)
+
+\method{RunIREA}{default}(
+  object,
+  reference,
+  contrast = NULL,
+  analysis = c("cytokine_response", "cell_polarization"),
+  method = c("score", "hypergeometric"),
+  gene_diff_cutoff = 0.25,
+  fdr_scope = c("all_contrasts", "selected_contrast"),
+  ...
+)
+
+\method{RunIREA}{Seurat}(
+  object,
+  reference,
+  group.by,
+  case,
+  control,
+  assay = "RNA",
+  layer = "data",
+  ...
+)
+}
+\arguments{
+\item{object}{Character gene vector, named numeric gene contrast vector,
+numeric one-column matrix (genes in rows), data frame with genes in its first
+column and numeric contrast columns, path to a .txt/.csv/.xls/.xlsx table,
+or a Seurat object. Positive contrasts mean higher expression in the case.
+Factors and text contrast columns are rejected. Missing/empty gene names and
+nonfinite contrasts are omitted; duplicated contrast gene names are rejected.}
+
+\item{...}{Arguments passed to the dispatched method.}
+
+\item{reference}{An \code{irea_reference} from \code{\link[=PrepareDB]{PrepareDB()}} or \code{\link[=PrepareIREA]{PrepareIREA()}}.}
+
+\item{contrast}{Column name to analyse in a table with multiple contrasts.}
+
+\item{analysis}{\code{"cytokine_response"} or \code{"cell_polarization"}.}
+
+\item{method}{\code{"score"} or \code{"hypergeometric"} for gene lists;
+numeric contrasts and Seurat inputs use cosine projection.}
+
+\item{gene_diff_cutoff}{Nonnegative minimum mean reference-gene expression for
+projection. Genes must exceed this value to be retained.}
+
+\item{fdr_scope}{\code{"all_contrasts"} adjusts BH jointly across reference terms
+and all supplied table columns before returning the selected contrast.
+\code{"selected_contrast"} adjusts its terms only. Vectors and Seurat inputs have
+one contrast. This option does not change gene-list calculations.}
+
+\item{group.by, case, control}{Metadata column and distinct group names for Seurat.}
+
+\item{assay, layer}{Seurat assay and exact expression layer name. Split layers
+must be joined first to compare cells across those layers.}
+}
+\value{
+An \code{irea_result} list, or the Seurat object with that result stored in
+\code{object@tools$IREA}. Other tools are preserved. The result contains:
+\itemize{
+\item \code{table}: one row per reference term, sorted by term. \code{effect} is the mean
+target-minus-PBS summed expression (score) or cosine projection difference,
+or the fraction of query genes in a significant signature (hypergeometric).
+\code{p_value} is a two-sided asymptotic Wilcoxon P value with continuity/tie
+correction, or an upper-tail hypergeometric P value; \code{fdr} is BH-adjusted.
+Empty target/control samples yield NA P/FDR. \code{status} uses FDR < 0.05.
+Score/projection include \code{n_target} and \code{n_control}; hypergeometric includes
+\code{overlap}, \code{set_size}, \code{query_size} and \code{universe_size}.
+Polarization adds \code{radar_score} between 0 and 1: zero if no positive effect has
+FDR < 0.05, otherwise each positive effect divided by the largest positive
+effect, with negative effects set to zero.
+\item \code{matched_genes}, \code{input_genes}: gene identifiers in input order after
+matching/filtering and before mapping, respectively.
+\item \code{gene_mapping}: human/mouse signature pairs for human input, otherwise NULL.
+\item \code{parameters}: analysis choices, exact selected contrast and FDR family;
+Seurat results additionally record grouping, case/control and assay/layer.
+\item \code{reference}: source paths, corresponding checksums and provenance.
+\item \code{validation}: experimental numerical-validation status.
+}
+}
+\description{
+Experimental reconstruction of published IREA score,
+hypergeometric and cosine-projection analyses. Numerical equivalence to the
+portal has not been established for all modes.
+}
+\details{
+Score and projection compare target reference cells against
+PBS-treated reference cells, including polarization. Their P values describe
+reference-cell distributions, not biological-replicate inference about the
+user's samples. Reference RNA data and the selected input layer must already
+be normalized. Human symbols are mapped to mouse reference genes using the
+signature tables; ambiguous and unmapped symbols are omitted.
+}
+\examples{
+\dontrun{
+reference <- PrepareDB(
+  db = "IREA_Macrophage", species = "Homo_sapiens"
+)[["Homo_sapiens"]][["IREA_Macrophage"]]
